   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">It looks like the problem is misaligned parentheses in the first return statement.  The return() closes after the log(sigma), leaving the &quot;<span style=\"font-size:12.8px;text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;float:none;display:inline\">- (1 + inv_xi) * log(z1)&quot; dangling.</span><div><span style=\"font-size:12.8px;text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;float:none;display:inline\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:12.8px;text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial;float:none;display:inline\">I know the error message did not explain the error precisely and left a blank after the colon. On the other hand, it did point to the problematic piece of code.  We hope to find time for some major improvements in the compiler, including better error trapping.  If anyone on this list is interested in contributing to this effort, please let me know.</span></div><div><br></div><div>Many kinds of programming errors can be tracked down by running a nimbleFunction uncompiled, directly in R.  Unfortunately R is not bothered by the offending syntax in this case.</div><div><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Thu, Jun 7, 2018 at 12:53 PM, Jeremy G Colman <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jgco...@sheffield.ac.uk</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I am getting a fatal error when my model with my user-defined distribution is compiling with nimbleMCMC. The error message is:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"> Error: In sizeAssignAfterRecursing: &#39;return&#39; is not available or its output type is unknown.</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"> This occurred for: Interm_21 &lt;- return(-((pow(z1,(-(inv_xi))))) - log(ARG3_sigma_))</blockquote><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"> This was part of the call: </blockquote></blockquote><div><br></div><div>Nothing follows the final colon.</div><div><br></div><div>My user-defined distribution is intended to be the generalised extreme value distribution. It is slightly complicated because the support of the distribution depends on the shape parameter, xi, so that if xi &lt; 0, the support is bounded above, if xi &gt; 0, it is bounded below, and if xi = 0 it is unbounded.</div><div><br></div><div>Here is my function:</div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204)